    "lawCode": "Amd §434, Judy L",
    "lawSection": "Judiciary Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5737\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the judiciary law, in relation to publication of\nofficial reports\n\nThis measure is being introduced at the request of the Chief\nAdministrative Judge on the recommendation of State Reporter{1}.\n\nThis measure would amend provisions of the Judiciary Law to change the\ntimetable for the bidding and award of the contract for publishing the\nJudiciary's Official Reports. The Official Reports include the\npublished decisions of the Court of Appeals (\"New York Reports\"), the\nAppellate Divisions (\"Appellate Division Reports\") and the Supreme\nCourt and other trial courts (\"Miscellaneous Reports\"). See Judiciary\nLaw § 433-a.\n\nThe measure is necessary to recognize the predominant role that\ntechnology now fills in the publication process.\n\nUnder present law, contracts for publication of the Official Reports\nare let under the direction of the State Reporter (Judiciary Law §\n434(2)) for periods of five years (id., § 434(7)). In each year\npreceding expiration of a contract, the State Reporter is required, at\nleast once in June, July and August, to commence the bidding process\nfor a successor contract by publishing notice in newspapers in New\nYork, Albany, Rochester and Buffalo that a new contract will be let,\nand that, on or before September first, sealed proposals for the\nsuccessor contract may be submitted. Thereafter, the contract must be\nlet between October 16th and November 1s1 and commence on January 1st\nof the next year.\n\nThis timetable was instituted many years ago - when the Official\nReports were only published in a print version. Today, it is\ninsufficient to permit an orderly transition of both print and\nelectronic publication processes in the event a successor contract is\nto be awarded to a new publisher. Thus, this measure proposes\namendments to the Judiciary Law: (1) to allow for additional\ntransition time by moving commencement of the bidding process from the\nfirst week of June to the first week of January in the year\nimmediately preceding the expiration of the contract, and (2) to save\nthe cost of classified newspaper advertisements for bid notices by\nrequiring that the request for bids on a new contract be published\nexclusively in the State's Procurement Opportunities Newsletter.\n\nTo maintain the current quality and timeliness of the Official\nReports, the Law Reporting Bureau publishing process relies heavily on\nautomation and computer programming. Transition to a new publisher for\nthe Official Reports would require acquisition, programming and\ninstallation of a new office-wide computer system, re-writing\nessential computer programs to accommodate the new publisher's\nproduction system and tests of the equipment and programs sufficiently\nin advance of the January first effective date of a new contract{2}.\nEven if an award is made and all necessary contract approvals are\nobtained from the Attorney General and the Office of the State\nComptroller at the earliest possible time, transition under the\ncurrent time frames established by the Judiciary Law would disrupt the\nLaw Reporting Bureau's operations for several months, result in\novertime costs for technology and other personnel during the\ntransition period and pose a significant risk to uninterrupted\nproduction of the online version of the Official Reports and the\nOfficial Reports advance sheets and bound volumes in accordance with\nweekly publication schedules established to comply with the Judiciary\nLaw.\n\nUnder the current procurement time frame and deadlines for the\npublishing contract, in the year in which bids for a successor\ncontract are to be received, the contract cannot practicably be let\nany earlier than October 16 nor any later than November first. In the\nperiod of 61 to 77 days between these dates and January first, when\nthe successor contract must take effect, that contract must be signed\nby the parties and the Chief Judge, and then approved by the Attorney\nGeneral and the Office of the State Comptroller. The time required by\nthese oversight agencies to approve can be considerable, and, as the\nState Reporter has advised, the entire process is usually not\ncompleted before late November. Accordingly, we recommend that the\nrelevant statutory provisions be amended to commence the bid process\nduring the first week of January of the year immediately preceding the\nexpiration of the contract and the remaining date provisions be\nadjusted to reflect the new commencement date. This should facilitate\na more efficient and timely completion of the bid letting process and\nimplementation of the successor contract. And, with elimination of an\nantiquated requirement for publication of advertisements for bid\nnotices in newspapers, it would save the State the costs associated\ntherewith{3}.\n\nThis act, which would take effect immediately, would produce some\nsmall savings for the State.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone. New proposal.\n\n{1} The State Reporter is appointed by the Court of Appeals. He or she\nheads the State's Law Reporting Bureau. See Judiciary Law § 430.\n\n{2} In connection with the bidding process for the 2016-2020 contract,\nrepresentatives of a major legal publisher acknowledged that a\npublisher transition under the current statutory timetable would be\nexceedingly difficult, and stated that a six-month period would best\nallow for the necessary preparation and testing and best assure\nuninterrupted publication during a transition.\n\n{3} Article 4-C of the State's Economic Development Law already\nrequires notice of bids to be published in the State's Procurement\nOpportunities Newsletter. There is no cost for this publication.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
